  "slug": "untitled-topic-29c0",
  "summary": "New preprints add noise models, confidence estimates and noise-corrected metrics to perturbation-response and spatial transcriptomics prediction.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that predictive omics models are being built and judged with explicit attention to measurement noise and reliability. For perturbation response, the Virtual Cell Challenge 2026 defines six metrics for zero-shot prediction of CRISPRi transcriptional responses. DeepCINET models viability assay noise in cancer cell line drug response ranking, and siGMA uses heteroscedastic uncertainty to handle noisy cross-dataset siRNA efficacy labels. GAIN predicts combinatorial single-cell responses as an additive baseline plus a learned interaction term. A parallel strand addresses imaging-based spatial transcriptomics. Dancer separates within-cell-type heterogeneity from transcript contamination, and SYNDRI, trained on simulated data, removes transcript spillover. For histology-to-expression prediction, REGAIN attaches per-prediction confidence, and a noise-corrected correlation metric (RSC) is proposed for benchmarking. BRIDGE (cross-tissue imputation on GTEx) and GASTON-VI (spatial gradients across samples) are adjacent. All are preprints, so reported gains remain unreviewed.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
